ARITHMETIC OPERATORS
Java arithmetic operators are symbols used to perform standard mathematical calculations on numeric primitive data types like int, double, and float. 
They are divided into binary operators (which require two values) and unary operators (which require only one value)

DEMO PROGRAM:

public class arithmeticoperators
{
    public static void main(String args[])
    {
         int n1 = 7;
         int n2 = 9;
         int sum = n1+n2;
         int difference = n1-n2;
         int product = n1*n2;
         double reminder = n1 % n2;
         double quotient = n1/n2;
    System.out.println("n1 is: "+n1+"\n");
    System.out.println("n2 is: "+n2+"\n");
    System.out.println("sum is: "+sum+"\n");    
    System.out.println("difference is: "+difference+"\n");   
    System.out.println("product is: "+product+"\n");
    System.out.println("reminder is: "+reminder+"\n");
    System.out.println("quotient is: "+quotient+"\n");

        int preincrement= ++n1;        //increment the value by one and then fetch the value
        int postincrement = n1++;     //fetch the value and then increments
    System.out.println("post incremented value is:"+postincrement+"\n");
    System.out.println("pre incremented value is:"+preincrement+"\n");
}
}
